From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent 9999: steer the MLD, not the link that happens to be first

A Client Steering Request for an MLO client has never produced a BTM frame.
Measured on hardware 2026-08-03 with a three-link client on ap-mld-1:

	ubus call map.controller steer '{"agent":"86:f7:56:41:01:d3",
	    "sta":"7a:6f:6d:db:82:92","target_agent":"e6:a9:d2:c1:98:3f",
	    "target_bssid":"00:0c:43:26:60:10","src_bssid":"7e:04:1c:81:7c:2e",
	    "abridged":1,"disassoc_tmo":0}'
	-> {"status":"ok","mid":2828}

	dump_steer_summary: btm_attempts 1, btm_success 0, btm_fail 0

Neither success nor failure - silence. The client did not move.

The request is correct on the wire. Captured at the agent:

	8014 ... 9b 001b 7e041c817c2e a0 0000 0000
	     01 7a6f6ddb8292      <- num_sta=1, the client MLD address
	     01 000c43266010      <- num_bss=1, the target BSSID
	     53 01                <- opclass 83, channel 1

and agent_process_steer_request_tlv() parses it correctly - the log prints
"sta_mac: 7a:6f:6d:db:82:92". What fails is the lookup that follows.

1. agent_set_sta_resp() compares the requested address against s->macaddr for
every station on ap->stalist. For an MLO client that list holds the address of
the affiliated link it associated on - 4a:d4:f5:d9:3e:1e here, the 2.4 GHz link
- while the controller names the client by its MLD address, which is what
EasyMesh R6 requires it to send. The two halves never meet, found stays false,
the function returns -1, and the caller goes straight to send_ack. The 1905 Ack
goes out, agent_try_steer_sta() is never reached, and no BTM frame is ever
built. struct sta already carries mld_macaddr and is_affiliated_sta, so the
comparison only has to look.

2. agent_request_btm() then sends through ap->ifname. On an MLD node that is
the per-link name and wifi.ap.<per-link> is published for no BSS - the same
condition 9997 ran into for statistics. request_btm lives on the MLD object:

	ubus -v list wifi.ap.ap-mld-1   -> request_btm present
	ubus -v list wifi.apmld.ap-mld-1 -> absent

So even with the lookup repaired the frame would have gone to an object that
does not exist. Both had to move for one BTM frame to leave the box.

Also fixed: the "not found" line printed s->macaddr, but s is the
list_for_each_entry iterator, and after a loop that ended without break it
points at the container of the list head. It was printing memory next to the
head - d7:d8:d5:d6:d3:d4 on this box, an address that appears nowhere on the
wire. That message sent this investigation down a wrong path for an hour; it
now prints the address actually looked for.

Not fixed here: the client is still held under the link address rather than
being indexed by both. Steering works after this, but anything else that looks
a client up by MLD address has the same trap waiting. Nor does this give the
agent per-link metrics - affiliated_sta.rcpi, dl_rate and ul_rate are empty in
the database, so RCPI-based steering policy still has nothing to decide on.

--- a/src/agent_map.c
+++ b/src/agent_map.c
@@ -4614,6 +4614,15 @@
 			agnt_dbg(LOG_STA, "%s: sta: " MACFMT "\n",
 			    __func__, MAC2STR(s->macaddr));
 			res = memcmp(s->macaddr, &sta_list[i * 6], 6);
+#if (EASYMESH_VERSION >= 6)
+			/* An MLO client is held here under the address of the
+			 * link it associated on, while the controller names it
+			 * by its MLD address - that is what the Steering
+			 * Request TLV carries, per spec. Compare both or the
+			 * two halves never meet. */
+			if (res && s->is_affiliated_sta)
+				res = memcmp(s->mld_macaddr, &sta_list[i * 6], 6);
+#endif
 			if (!res) {
 				found = true;
 				/**
@@ -4630,8 +4639,12 @@
 			}
 		}
 		if (!found) {
+			/* s is the iterator: after the loop ran to its end it
+			 * points at the container of the list head, not at an
+			 * entry, so printing s->macaddr showed memory next to
+			 * the head. Print what was actually looked for. */
 			agnt_dbg(LOG_STA, "%s: sta: " MACFMT " not found\n",
-			    __func__, MAC2STR(s->macaddr));
+			    __func__, MAC2STR(&sta_list[i * 6]));
 			memcpy(sta_resp[count].sta_mac, &sta_list[i * 6], 6);
 			sta_resp[count].response = ERR_REASON_STA_NOT_ASSOCIATED;
 			count++;
@@ -4843,6 +4856,13 @@
 
 	req.validity_int = BTM_VALIDITY_TM_MSEC / WIFI_BEACON_PERIOD_MS;
 	req.dialog_token = agent_steer_next_dialog_token(a);
+#if (EASYMESH_VERSION >= 6)
+	/* wifi.ap.<per-link> is published for no BSS on an MLD node; the
+	 * request_btm method lives on wifi.ap.<mld_ifname>. Same reason
+	 * agent_fetch_ap_stats() addresses the MLD entity. */
+	if (ap->is_affiliated_ap && ap->mld_ifname[0])
+		return wifi_req_btm(ap->mld_ifname, client_sta, 1, pref, &req);
+#endif
 	return wifi_req_btm(ap->ifname, client_sta, 1, pref, &req);
 }
 
